Fix valid C++ record copies and bit casts being rejected as reads before initialization when private helpers receive only the fields they use. Also stop discarded non-volatile references from requesting values that C++ does not read.

Private reference calls deliberately omit unused fields. The analysis that selects those arguments missed reads performed by trivial record construction and `__builtin_bit_cast`; translation then tried to consume an operand that the caller had omitted. Discarded-value casts had the opposite mismatch: analysis correctly omitted a read, but translation fetched the discarded value anyway.

## Source behavior

These ordinary constructor and copy forms now retain their source values through member calls:

```cpp
struct Coordinates { int values[3]; };
struct Bounds {
  Coordinates lower, upper;
  Bounds(const Coordinates& first, const Coordinates& last) : lower(first), upper{last} {}
};
```

The same rule covers `Pair(value)`, `Pair{value}`, and local `Pair copy{value}` initialization. Scalar and vector bit casts retain their operands as well:

```cpp
static unsigned scalar_bits(const float& value) {
  return __builtin_bit_cast(unsigned, value);
}
```

```loom
func.def @scalar_bits(%value: f32) -> (i32) {
  %bits = scalar.bitcast %value : f32 to i32
  func.return %bits : i32
}
```

Discarding a reference preserves the narrow signature:

```cpp
struct Pair { int x, y; };
static int first(const Pair& p, const Pair& q) {
  (void)q;
  static_cast<void>(q.y);
  return p.x;
}
```

```loom
func.def @first(%p_x: i32) -> (i32) {
  func.return %p_x : i32
}
```

Neither `p.y` nor either field of `q` becomes an argument. Discarded subscripts, reference-returning calls, casts, and conditional selections still evaluate their increments, calls, and selected branches exactly once. Volatile observations retain their loads.

## Implementation

Record copy and bit-cast reads are added to the existing control-flow analysis traversal. Parenthesized and braced construction share a single same-type copy check; reference binding remains distinct from copying a value. Discarded expressions use the existing effect translation and conditional-region machinery instead of materializing unused referents. There is no new pass, core IR representation, or whole-function traversal.

Target-independent importer fixtures check complete signatures, operand wiring, copy payloads, and evaluation effects. Existing volatile and inherited-operator fixtures continue checking their observable behavior. The pipeline rejection fixture now performs an actual workitem-ID read, preserving its check that topology queries require a strand body.

Fixes #3020.

Fixes https://github.com/matbun/kueue-offload/issues/8 and https://github.com/matbun/kueue-offload/issues/12. Part of https://github.com/matbun/kueue-offload/issues/4, https://github.com/matbun/kueue-offload/issues/6 and https://github.com/matbun/kueue-offload/issues/10.

This brings the Kueue path to parity with the virtual-node setup and adds what that setup cannot do:

- one virtual node per site: every offloaded workload gets pods there, with `kubectl logs` and `kubectl exec` served by the executor and the plugin;
- pod groups, TrainJobs, JobSets and Ray owners end together as one allocation, also when a member is deleted;
- a deferred attempt releases its quota at once and waits `deferInterval` before the next;
- projects and queues as a Cohort tree: a Cohort per project holds its quota and lends nothing, each queue of a site gets a ClusterQueue and a LocalQueue in every member namespace, and `membership.admissionFairSharing` orders the members of a project by their recent use;
- membership: executor templates in parts as kustomize generates them, uninstall in two GitOps steps.

`0.1.0-dev.27` is this branch's head. Every suite of the validation kit passed on it on 2026-11-10 at two sites, Slurm over ssh and FirecREST: pod groups, TrainJobs (torch, MPI, TF), JobSet, Ray, Katib, a KFP step, notebooks, staging, and the projects and queues scenario. Fair sharing needs a Kueue that settles the entry penalty of a workload admitted through an admission check (https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kueue/issues/12539).

